WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.853
Fossil fuels, you know the drill.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:10.660
They are the main drivers
of the global economy.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.320
In the most fundamental way possible,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.460
hydrocarbons and their use

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:20.601
has undoubtedly improved
human welfare enormously.

00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:22.470
And at the same time,

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.770
they're the primary
cause of global warming

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:27.770
that's dangerously altering
the Earth's climate.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:29.980
The expansion and development

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.060
of new oil reserves is inconsistent

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.710
with a safe climate in the future.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.230
The tension between economic

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:40.770
and environmental priorities
couldn't be clearer

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.653
than in the fight for
and against pipelines.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:46.610
Biden's decision to revoke a permit

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:50.200
for Keystone XL will certainly
be a topic of discussion.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.350
It marks the end of a saga spanning more

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.690
than 12 years over the pipeline meant

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.293
to carry Canadian crude
to the United States.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.480
Pipelines have come to
symbolize this battle

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:05.230
in society between oil
and gas development,

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:09.170
and a switch to controlling climate change

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:12.850
towards developing other
alternative renewable sources

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:14.150
of energy.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.070
Pipeline industry is kind of a barometer

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:18.300
of where things are going.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.300
Has gone from alive to dead,
to alive, to dead again.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:24.970
It seems likely that the days

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:28.180
of these $5 billion plus 1,000 mile

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.203
long projects are over.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.060
This is the story

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.930
of the billion dollar business built

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:35.670
on the back of fossil fuels

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.181
and how it too may go
the way of the dinosaurs.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:51.540
There's no doubt that oil

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.673
and gas made the world what it is today.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.980
Once reliant on foreign oil predominantly

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.000
in the Middle East,

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.570
the US became closer
to its perennial quest

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.090
for energy independence
within the last decade

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.740
with advances in fracking.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.690
About 10 years ago is the start

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.240
of what we consider the shale revolution.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.760
And that's when we combined
hydraulic fracturing

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.150
and horizontal drilling,

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.640
and we just unleashed this record amount

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.400
of US oil and gas supply.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.320
The shale revolution has had its own ups

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.820
and downs, but regardless of fluctuations

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.420
in supply and demand,

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.740
we always need to move this stuff around.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:34.760
To support this massive growth
in oil and gas production,

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.530
you need this suitably
large underground network

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.790
of pipelines to move those products

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:43.500
to export terminals, to power plants,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.260
to refineries, and
petrochemical facilities.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:47.860
These are not small ventures.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.610
They're thousands of miles long

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:51.410
just for one pipeline alone.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:53.050
They cost billions of dollars.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.510
They require massive permitting
at the federal level.

00:02:58.485 --> 00:03:00.840
And so it was that the
shale revolution led

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.780
to a pipeline boom.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.150
But that boom ultimately
triggered an explosion

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.744
of lawsuits broadly led
by environmentalists.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.520
No project embodies the
Herculean nature and divisiveness

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:16.163
of this business better than
the Keystone XL pipeline.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.670
The Keystone XL pipeline
is like a diagonal line

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.633
from the middle of Alberta
down to Houston, Texas.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:27.150
It cuts through the US states of Montana,

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.170
South Dakota, Nebraska, Kansas,

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.053
Oklahoma, and Texas.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:36.570
A project of this scale faces
varying permitting systems

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:37.760
from state to state,

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.250
as well as multiple run-ins

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.920
with a host of conflicting
interest groups.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.650
The Keystone XL pipeline embodied a lot

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:49.390
of the elements that
pipeline opponents hated

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.550
so much about the industry.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:55.270
It was massive, you know, more
than a thousand miles long.

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:58.140
It carried oil from the Alberta oil sands

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.130
which a lot of folks called tar sands

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:03.290
because it's a particularly
heavy grade of crude.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:07.010
It went through private
land, indigenous land.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.960
It wasn't even carrying
American oil and gas.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.460
So, I think a lot of
folks saw it as something

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:15.297
that represented everything they hated

00:04:15.297 --> 00:04:18.113
about the fossil fuel
extraction industries.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:23.500
This is environmental
activist Anthony Swift

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:26.000
from the Natural
Resources Defense Council,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.800
and suffice it to say, he's
not a fan of oil pipelines.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:34.800
Keystone XL was a 830,000
barrel a day tar sands pipeline

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.720
that would've expanded
the dirtiest source of oil

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:39.420
in the world for decades to come,

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:41.260
a scenario that would've been inconsistent

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:42.280
with a safe climate.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:43.580
And in addition to that,

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:45.180
it would've had devastating impacts

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:48.640
to indigenous communities
in Alberta and communities

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.240
along the route who rely

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.500
on water sources
threatened by the pipeline.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.180
Proposed in 2005,

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.840
the $5.2 billion project pressed forward

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.470
with relative ease and
with the cooperation

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:03.123
of the US government under
the Bush administration.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.600
This is Dennis McConaghy,

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.800
a former executive at TC Energy,

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.330
the pipeline company behind Keystone.

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:11.410
As you could imagine,

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.820
he has some strong opinions
about the industry,

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:16.890
environmentalists, and
the Biden administration.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.860
The regulatory process under
the Bush administration

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.290
for base Keystone went very easily.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.390
It was all done within almost two years.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:25.860
The United States needed heavy oil.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:29.310
We had it to sell and we
were the pipeline conduit

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.720
that was gonna create this system

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.540
that would create the greatest
transportation economy

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:36.480
for Canadian oil sands producers.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.470
So, rather than sending
it out on trains, trucks,

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:43.050
this high-volume, direct link was going

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.930
to give us the greatest
transportation economy.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:49.270
This project became
resisted by, what I'll say,

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:52.660
basically the North American
environmental movement.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:53.950
You know, the reality is,

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:56.220
there is no conceivable way

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.040
that we can continue
to use the same amount

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.500
of oil we're using today

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.640
without creating
catastrophic climate impacts.

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:04.950
The reign of oil has to come

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:08.620
to a close if we're going
to have a safe climate.

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:10.910
And fortunately, we have the replacements

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:12.973
and alternatives available today.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:17.010
Environmental groups
did win some victories

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.680
under the Obama administration,

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.323
especially when it came to
the pipeline battlefront.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.860
In 2014, Obama vetoed a bill

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.170
in favor of moving the pipeline forward.

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:28.170
Then the following year,

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:30.230
after six years of consideration,

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:33.003
he decided to reject the
project's permit outright.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.420
Because Keystone XL was going

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:38.170
to cross the Canadian-US border,

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:40.600
it needed special approval
from the State Department,

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.700
which is not something
that pipelines that just go

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.740
from one state to another
are required here in the US.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.500
That gives the White House more power

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:50.950
than it would normally

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:55.010
to either approve or
kill a pipeline project.

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:56.730
The State Department has decided

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:59.560
that the Keystone XL pipeline

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.590
would not serve the national
interests of the United States.

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:06.690
Let me just say that life
has a way of surprising us.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:08.640
What a great honor to be able to introduce

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:11.340
for the first time ever, anywhere,

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:15.960
the 45th president of the
United States of America,

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:17.353
Donald J. Trump.

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:26.560
Trump, giving them a permit in early 2017.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:27.790
Keystone pipeline.

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:29.700
Legal interventions caused that permit

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:33.250
to have to be reissued back in 2019.

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:34.920
And it took that length of time

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.180
before TransCanada and its shippers,

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.040
who had to be reassembled commercially,

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.123
started to construct the project in 2020.

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.260
The pendulum, once again,

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:45.900
swung in the other direction when,

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:47.670
shortly after his inauguration,

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.980
President Biden halted the
pipeline's Phase IV construction,

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:53.150
which was to cut over 500 miles

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:55.750
and run straight through
a national landmark area

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:58.390
in Nebraska called the Sand Hills.

00:07:58.390 --> 00:08:02.110
I'm visiting you from Oyen, Alberta,

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:05.230
and until a few days ago,
this was kind of ground zero

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:08.500
for construction of the
Keystone XL pipeline.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:11.720
That all pretty much came
to a halt a few days ago.

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:14.240
This was something that was
enormously controversial.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:17.240
It had this kind of outsized
impact on the way we talk

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:20.072
about our energy and climate in the US.

00:08:20.072 --> 00:08:22.760
It was something that he
promised to do and he did it.

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:25.680
I think it was seen as kind of an easy way

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:27.810
for Biden to quickly deliver on some

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.380
of his environmental promises

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:31.310
because, at the end of the day,

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:33.460
this was a pipeline
that was not yet built.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:35.510
It is a lot harder to go after something

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:38.790
that has 200 miles of steel
already in the ground.

00:08:38.790 --> 00:08:41.749
Biden's cancellation of the
Keystone XL project was,

00:08:41.749 --> 00:08:44.410
you know, an absolutely
necessary decision.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:48.470
It upheld the decision that
President Obama made in 2016

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:49.970
after a thorough review

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:51.970
and it just shows that he's serious

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:55.520
about transitioning the US to clean energy

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.870
and really taking a leadership stance

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:01.100
on climate change globally.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:02.200
But focusing their energy

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:04.100
on the pipelines is often seen

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:07.340
by people in the industry
as misdirected activism.

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:09.650
This doesn't stop oil sands production.

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:12.410
It doesn't stop Canadians
trying to find other ways

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:15.120
of getting that oil
into the United States,

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:18.310
but it does reduce the economic value

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:20.780
of having lost the most efficient option

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:22.143
that you could've resorted.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:27.280
When we think about the impact

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:30.050
that the Keystone XL
pipeline would have had

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:31.030
on the oil market,

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:35.210
I think is when we start
to see even opinions

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:37.650
in the energy industry divided.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:39.990
There are signs that there is a shortage

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:43.190
of so-called takeaway
capacity out of Alberta.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:46.980
However, on the other side,
it's not clear that we needed,

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:47.870
you know, a pipeline

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:52.660
that could shuttle 800,000
barrels a day plus out of Canada.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:55.120
So, I think the impact and the fallout

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:57.566
from this decision is
going to remain to be seen.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.300
A major point that pro-pipeline advocates

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:03.140
emphasize is of course jobs.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:05.440
And while it's true that
killing a pipeline does result

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:06.810
in a loss of some work,

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:10.150
that might be a rather
myopic definition of jobs.

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:12.170
As far as jobs, the industry has said

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:15.170
that it would've employed
over 10,000 direct jobs.

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:17.760
Obviously, that would
have a pretty big impact

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:19.870
on certain communities
and in certain circles

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:22.453
where there are a lot
of industrial workers.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:25.520
I think it's important to remember

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:28.180
that these were not permanent positions.

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:30.770
These were mostly construction jobs

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:34.510
and pipeline development
jobs on the ground.

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:36.860
That's a little bit
more complicated picture

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:39.923
than I think the industry
has made it sound.

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:44.470
There really are several
different economic interests

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:46.490
who were impacted by this.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:49.750
So, there are the people
like TransCanada Energy

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:52.440
and their other partners in the pipeline,

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:56.040
notably the Alberta government,
the province of Alberta.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:58.500
You know, they stand to lose
somewhere my guess would be

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:00.570
on the order of $4 billion.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:03.810
All of that is kind of like
the sunk cost of just trying

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:05.250
to do the project.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:08.780
States that lose some
of the economic benefits

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:10.200
that derive from the project,

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.360
such as property tax payments,

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:13.580
that literally would rank

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:16.300
in the billions for states like Montana,

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:18.430
South Dakota, Nebraska.

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:20.290
Those states would also have benefited

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:23.190
from the construction activity
that would've occurred

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:24.860
in their states that's lost.

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:27.130
Tradesmen that had jobs working

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:29.240
on this pipeline that don't exist anymore.

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:31.780
The American refining interests

00:11:31.780 --> 00:11:35.870
who were seeing value in
running Canadian crude

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:38.310
from a security point
of view or from the fact

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:40.420
that it just would work
better than other sources,

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:42.643
whether those were from
Venezuela or Mexico.

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:47.040
President Biden's American Jobs Plan

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:51.140
has earmarked $174 billion
to win the EV market

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:53.780
and another $34 billion for solar, wind,

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:56.180
and other alternative energy solutions.

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:57.690
If this becomes a reality,

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:01.090
the laws of supply and demand
may drastically shift away

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:03.620
from fossil fuels forever

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:07.790
Biden's Job Plan is a really
important foundational step

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:10.610
in building an economy that's on the basis

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:13.770
of clean energy and
supports the just transition

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:17.730
of workers from efforts
to expand oil production,

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:20.280
contrary to our health and safety,

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.320
to projects that allow us to clean it up.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:24.560
Demands could be nearing a peak.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.550
Whether it be electrification of vehicles,

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:29.640
demand for oil will stop growing,

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:32.240
development of resources in the oil sands.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:34.410
The oil here will keep being pumped out,

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:35.810
but there won't be as much need

00:12:35.810 --> 00:12:39.140
to expand oil production in Canada.

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:42.023
The demand for new pipelines will be less.

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:44.900
Ultimately, with the complexity

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:46.480
of getting these pipelines built

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.670
and the political maelstroms around them,

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:51.973
the pipeline boom may very
well turn into a bust.

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:57.120
Fossil fuels are a controversial
topic today in the US.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.180
If you are building a pipeline

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:01.850
that shuttles them from
one place to another

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:05.240
and you're doing that by
claiming eminent domain

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:07.930
so that you can take
someone else's property,

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:09.300
you better be pretty sure

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:10.960
that you can do it without getting sued.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:11.940
And that if you are sued,

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:14.470
you're successful because
every month that goes

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:16.610
by where that project is delayed

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:19.770
or there is some other legal
development that arises,

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:22.130
that's adding costs to your project

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:24.270
and that is the absolute opposite

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:26.780
of what these investors wanna see.

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:29.240
It's just been dramatic

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:33.930
how quickly these new
projects had to basically stop

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:37.370
in the face of all of this
environmental opposition.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:39.230
You see companies like Williams

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:42.460
who tried to build not
one, but two gas pipelines

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:44.700
in the Northeast and got shut down

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:47.230
by the state of New York both times.

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:49.940
Energy transfer with the
Dakota Access Pipeline,

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:51.410
the Rover gas pipeline,

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:54.160
both of those encountered
tremendous opposition

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:55.300
from environmental groups,

00:13:55.300 --> 00:14:00.070
but even from regulators
themselves, even in Texas,

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:01.880
which we never would've expected

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:04.110
to see very much opposition here.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:06.130
Kinder Morgan had trouble getting

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:08.220
a natural gas pipeline built recently.

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:10.020
So, I think it's just becoming clear

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:12.020
that this is not an industry that's going

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:13.550
to be able to just grow and grow,

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:15.150
and grow like it used to be.

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:16.990
There's still people who think

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:21.320
that Keystone XL will be revived maybe

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:23.250
with a new administration.

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:25.340
It's a bit uncertain
how much need there is

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:26.370
for the pipeline.

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:29.520
Will there be even the
impetus to drive ahead?

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:30.820
It's a pretty clear win

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:32.180
for the environmental movement.

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:34.690
This is something that
they have sought to kill

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:37.300
for over a decade now.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:39.940
The American environmental movement won

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:44.140
because it attached itself
to the democratic candidate,

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:47.080
that person won, he followed
through on his commitment

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:48.700
that he would deny this project.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:51.510
Biden's policy goals are set

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:54.520
by an international
community that is aligned

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:56.910
at maintaining a safe climate.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:58.560
And that means that projects

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.610
like Keystone XL are simply
inconsistent consistent

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:01.490
with that goal.

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:03.080
And the environmental community is aligned

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:05.060
with the Biden administration's goal

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:06.850
of maintaining a safe climate

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:10.010
and that was what the
president was elected

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:11.793
to achieve by the American people.

00:15:13.430 --> 00:15:16.250
When we look at the pipeline
industry as a whole,

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:18.340
I think it's a little bit more nuanced.

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:22.330
For some companies, Keystone XL being dead

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:24.130
actually might save them

00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:27.270
from some more sweeping
environmental regulations

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:30.080
that would've impacted their own projects.

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:31.460
At the end of the day,

00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:35.030
I think the fate of the
pipeline industry was sealed

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:36.810
before Keystone XL.

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:39.310
We're probably going to be consuming fewer

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:42.980
and fewer barrels of oil,
less and less natural gas

00:15:42.980 --> 00:15:46.260
as we progressed through the century.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:49.860
I just don't think
anybody, be it investors,

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:52.300
green groups, landowners,

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:56.490
they wanna see another
$7, $10 billion pipeline.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:59.700
And I think what that
means is that, for the US,

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:04.183
we're done building these
mega pipeline projects.

